  <p>The concept of continuity is closely related to limits. A function is said to be continuous at a point if the limit of the function as x approaches that point is equal to the value of the function at that point. In other words, a function f(x) is continuous at x=a if <span style="color: var(--success);">lim x→a f(x) = f(a)</span>. This concept is crucial in calculus, as it allows us to define the concept of a derivative, which is a measure of how a function changes as its input changes.</p>
